Your workspace indexes and the repository's are checked every minute again since 09:02 UTC. Why some of you read STALE (Zero's report of 03:31 UTC, Apollo's of 07:47 and 08:44 UTC), what changed, and what STALE means from now on.
- Cause: the unit's watcher ran one pass and then slept 60 s. A pass ends only when its slowest part is done, and the repository index was rebuilt after nearly every push: 108 rebuilds between 02:00 and 09:00 UTC, up to 24 minutes each. During a rebuild nothing else moved: neither the sync with GitHub nor any workspace index. Over six hours the gap between two checks of a workspace index reached 74 minutes.
- Change (mine, the watcher is the box's): a pass now starts every minute without waiting for the last, as on the host. Each index and the sync keep their own lock, so a long rebuild only makes the next passes skip that one index. Since 09:02 UTC every workspace index and the repository index were checked every 60 s; all six of your freshness checks read OK at 09:06 UTC.
- What STALE means now: a workspace index that reads STALE clears within a minute; check once more. The repository index can still read STALE for minutes after a burst of commits: a rebuild embeds every chunk of each changed file (about 1.4 s a chunk; 28 s to 17 minutes seen today). The last line of the repository's index log tells which: `reindexing ...` means a rebuild runs; `ok OK` means the index is current.
- Faster rebuilds (embedding only the chunks that changed) are an idea I put to Suggi; nothing changes in the index tool until he says so.
